UNIT wire_line
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@f68cca701417 cli/lang/rust.mjs wireLine with its helpers wireValue, notCarriable, jsonText, optionOf, ownKeys — the rust driver's argument transport after the round-5 rewrite (selfhost #93) and the round-5b narrowings (#119), reproduced exactly, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG wire_line(args: list[json], sig: json) -> text | record[__narrowed: text]
ENVELOPE args is the decoded argument list of one vector; sig is a parsed SIG tree (DESCRIPTION gives its shape). The js
  widening is the unit's real domain: arguments may hold big integers, byte arrays and host byte buffers, the absent
  value, lists with holes, text with lone surrogates (values and map keys), keyed collections, sets, regexps and boxed
  primitives; args may be a map, a text or none; sig may be a hand-built tree that breaks the SIG grammar. Throws are IN
  scope as observations (R2). OUT: callables and symbols as argument values, accessor properties, proxies, objects with a
  prototype other than the generic object's (the codec rebuilds every map on the generic prototype), holes in sig's
  parameter list. HOST KEY ORDER (R15, R17) is graded ONLY through the js extension: the shared oracle carries every map
  argument as a canonical map, so its shared vectors hold maps whose keys are already in sorted order, and every probe of
  another creation order is a js-extension vector.
DEPENDS-ON none
UNGUARDED-PRECONDITION sig.args assumes a list of parameter maps  DISCHARGE: caller
UNGUARDED-PRECONDITION every type node reached by a present value assumes a k field and the node fields its kind names  DISCHARGE: unchecked
DESCRIPTION Turn one vector's decoded argument list into the single line of JSON text the verifier's generated rust harness
  reads on stdin, walking each argument by its abstract type from the parsed SIG; or answer NARROWED — a fresh record
  whose one field __narrowed holds a REASON text — when the rust side could not receive the vector exactly as the js side
  does. A parsed SIG tree is a map: args = a list of parameter maps {name: text, type: NODE, rest: true only on a rest
  parameter}; name and result are present but never read. A NODE is a map whose k names the kind: text, bytes, bool, none,
  i64, u64, f64, json (no other field); list with item: NODE; tuple with items: list of NODE; map with key and val: NODE;
  record with fields: a list of [field name, NODE] pairs in declared order; union with alts: list of NODE in written order.
  Every rendering below is compact JSON text: no whitespace anywhere outside text literals. REASON texts are quoted below
  exactly; <n> is a count in decimal digits.
BEHAVIOR
R1 Reading the SIG. FIXED = the number of parameters not marked rest. REST = the type of the FIRST parameter marked rest;
  a rest parameter with no type (absent or none) means there is NO rest. Argument j (0-based) is wired at the type of the
  j-th entry of the WHOLE parameter list when j < FIXED (whether or not that entry is the rest one), else at REST.
  CITED FIDELITY package
R2 Throws, and only these, before any argument is looked at: sig is none or absent, sig has no parameter list (or one that
  is not a list), or a parameter entry is none. They throw even when args is malformed. Nothing else throws (R5).
R3 Shape. args not a list -> NARROWED "the args are not a list". No REST and the args count differs from FIXED ->
  NARROWED "<count> args for a <FIXED>-parameter SIG". With a REST any count passes, fewer than FIXED included (only the
  arguments present are wired). Then, before any argument is wired, a hole anywhere in args -> NARROWED HOLE_TEXT (it
  wins over every argument's own result, faults included). CITED FIDELITY package
R4 Walk. The arguments are wired left to right by WIRE (R6-R16); the FIRST argument whose wire is NARROWED is the result
  and no later argument is examined. Otherwise the result is "[" + the wires joined by "," + "]"; no arguments -> "[]".
R5 A host fault while wiring one argument (a malformed type node, TABLE MALFORMED) -> NARROWED "wire: " + the fault
  message. Inside one list, tuple or record EVERY element / item / field is wired before the first NARROWED among them is
  chosen, so a fault anywhere inside an argument wins over a NARROWED sibling in it; across arguments R4's order decides.
R6 None and absent first, at every typed position, any depth. The ABSENT value (an argument, list element or tuple item
  that is absent, or a declared record field the value lacks or holds as absent) is tested FIRST, by reading the node: at
  a none node or an OPTION union (R7) it wires "null"; at every other node (json, map, a multi-way union, a scalar, a kind
  outside the set, a node with no k) -> NARROWED ABSENT_REASON. Then a none value wires "null" before the type is read,
  whatever the type (malformed included). So on a malformed node absent can fault (TABLE MALFORMED) where none cannot.
  CITED FIDELITY package
R7 Union. An OPTION union — exactly two alternatives, exactly one of them not none, in either order — wires the value by
  that alternative's rule. Every other union (two non-none alternatives, three or more, one, or zero) wires the value by
  the json rule R15, whatever the alternatives are: the value is never checked against them. CITED FIDELITY package
R8 f64. A number -> its IEEE-754 binary64 bit pattern as a JSON string of exactly 16 lowercase hex digits, every NaN as
  7ff8000000000000 (-0 -> "8000000000000000", +Infinity -> "7ff0000000000000"). Anything else (a big integer included)
  -> NARROWED "not an f64 value".
R9 text. A text with a lone surrogate -> NARROWED "a lone surrogate has no UTF-8 form"; another text -> TEXT-ESCAPE.
  Anything else (a boxed text included) -> NARROWED "not a text value".
R10 bool. true / false -> "true" / "false"; anything else -> NARROWED "not a bool value".
R11 i64 and u64. Accepted: a big integer, or a number that is an integer of magnitude at most 2^53-1 (-0 counts, and is
  0); in range [-2^63, 2^63-1] for i64, [0, 2^64-1] for u64 -> its exact decimal digits ("-" when negative). Anything else
  (a fraction, 2^53 as a number, out of range, non-number) -> NARROWED "not a i64 value" / "not a u64 value".
  CITED FIDELITY package
R12 list. Not a list (a byte array, a text, a map: anything but a list) -> NARROWED "not a list value". A list with any
  hole -> NARROWED HOLE_TEXT (checked before any element is wired). Otherwise each element is wired at the item type;
  the first NARROWED element is the wire; else "[" + the element wires joined by "," + "]".
R13 tuple. A list with any hole -> NARROWED HOLE_TEXT, checked first (before the length test and before the node's items
  are read, so it wins over a wrong length and over a node with no items). Otherwise: not a list, or a list whose length
  differs from the item count -> NARROWED "not a <items>-tuple"; else item i is wired at item type i, first NARROWED
  wins, else "[...]" as R12. CITED FIDELITY package
R14 record. Not a plain map (a list, keyed collection, set, byte array, regexp, boxed value, scalar) -> NARROWED "not a
  record value". Otherwise each declared field, in declared order, wires the value's OWN property of that name at the
  field type — a missing one is absent (R6: "null" only at a none or option field; an inherited member such as
  constructor is never read). First NARROWED field wins; else "{" + each JSON-quoted field name (TEXT-ESCAPE) + ":" +
  its wire, joined by ",", + "}". Undeclared keys of the value are dropped unchecked.
R15 json and map (and R7's non-option unions). If the value is NOT CARRIABLE -> NARROWED with the first REASON found by
  CARRY; otherwise the value's PLAIN rendering. A map position never consults its key or value type: map[text, f64]
  holding "x" sends "x", a number as decimal (not bits). CARRY, depth first, elements in index order and map entries in
  HOST KEY ORDER (a key before its value):
  none, bool -> carriable;
  a number that is NaN, +Infinity, -Infinity or -0 -> NARROWED_JSON;
  a whole number of magnitude above 2^53 (2^53 itself passes) -> "a whole number past 2^53 reaches serde_json as a
  different integer" (whether or not its rendering would be exact);
  a big integer below -2^63 or above 2^64-1 -> "an integer past i64/u64 has no serde_json form";
  a text (or a map key) with a lone surrogate -> "a lone surrogate has no UTF-8 form";
  the absent value (nested) -> ABSENT_REASON;
  a list hole -> HOLE_TEXT;
  a plain map -> its entries; a list -> its elements;
  any other object -> "a <TAG> has no serde_json form", TAG = the host's class name: Uint8Array (a host byte buffer too),
  Map (keyed collection), Set, RegExp, Number, String, Boolean (boxed). CITED FIDELITY package
R16 Every other kind (bytes, none, a kind outside the closed set, a node with no k) -> NARROWED "the rust harness has no
  route for <k>", <k> the kind text as written, or ABSENT_TEXT when the node has no k. CITED FIDELITY package
R17 PLAIN rendering (only of CARRIABLE values): a big integer -> its decimal digits, unquoted; none -> "null"; bool ->
  "true" / "false"; a number -> NUMBER-TEXT (CARRY has removed every whole number past 2^53); text -> TEXT-ESCAPE; a
  list -> "[" + elements PLAIN joined by "," + "]"; a plain map -> "{" + each TEXT-ESCAPE key + ":" + value PLAIN,
  joined by ",", + "}" in HOST KEY ORDER: keys that are array indices (the canonical decimal spelling of an integer
  0 .. 2^32-2) first, ascending numerically, then every other key in creation order. CITED FIDELITY package
TABLE MALFORMED — the fault message R5 reports, for a value that is not none (R6 spares none only); <x> is ABSENT_TEXT when the field is missing, "null" when it is none
  the type node itself is missing or none, any value but none (absent included)     -> Cannot read properties of <x> (reading 'k')
  a union node with no alts, any value but none (absent included)                   -> Cannot read properties of <x> (reading 'filter')
  a list node with no item, at an element that is not none (absent included)        -> Cannot read properties of <x> (reading 'k')
  a tuple node with no items, a present value that is not a list with a hole        -> Cannot read properties of <x> (reading 'length')
  a record node with no fields, the value a plain map                               -> Cannot read properties of <x> (reading 'map')
TABLE TEXT-ESCAPE — the JSON string rendering of a well-formed text (UTF-16 code units, left to right), wrapped in double quotes; ESC = U+005C reverse solidus
  U+0022 quotation mark                                -> ESC then the quotation mark
  U+005C reverse solidus                               -> ESC then ESC
  U+0008 U+0009 U+000A U+000C U+000D                   -> ESC then the letter b, t, n, f, r respectively
  any other unit below U+0020                          -> ESC, the letter u, "00", two lowercase hex digits
  everything else (U+007F, U+2028, U+2029, a surrogate pair) -> itself, unescaped
TABLE NUMBER-TEXT — a finite number other than -0: zero -> "0"; a negative one -> "-" then its magnitude's text; a positive x, written with the fewest significant digits k that read back as x and decimal exponent n (x = digits × 10^(n-k))
  k <= n <= 21                     -> the digits followed by n-k zeros
  0 < n <= 21                      -> the first n digits, ".", the rest
  -6 < n <= 0                      -> "0." then -n zeros then the digits
  otherwise                        -> d[.ddd] then "e", then "+" or "-", then abs(n-1) (1e+21, 1e-7, 1.5e-7)
PINNED text ABSENT_TEXT = "undefined" CITED R16 — the host's spelling of a missing field, as R16 and TABLE MALFORMED render it
PINNED text ABSENT_REASON = "the absent value has no serde_json form" CITED R6 — the reason for the absent value where rust has no null for it
PINNED text HOLE_TEXT = "an array hole has no serde_json form" CITED R3 — the reason for a hole in args, a list, a tuple or a json list
PINNED text NARROWED_JSON = "json holds no NaN/Inf/-0 in serde_json::Value" CITED R15 — the driver's exported reason for a non-finite or negative-zero number under json
INVARIANT every text result begins with "[" and ends with "]" and is valid JSON.
INVARIANT NARROWED is never sent: it replaces the whole line.
ORACLE value -> oracles/wire_line.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 4e62fc80375dc0f717e0885958ed1119a8ef8ae61755a7c789ef8289dac9823d
NOTE No rust variant, by decision: the unit's input is the js host's own decoded values (big integers, byte buffers, the absent
  value, holes, lone surrogates, keyed collections) and the result depends on host accidents (host key order, class tags,
  host fault messages). It is the js side of the rust transport; a rust artifact would verify a shadow of it.
SCOPE IN: wireLine and the helpers it calls (wireValue, notCarriable, jsonText, optionOf, ownKeys, lone, narrowed) over the
  envelope, R1-R17. OUT: the harness generator, marshalling and parsing on the rust side (harnessMain, parseWire, reshape,
  batchCaller), and the rest of cli/lang/rust.mjs.
PROVENANCE rederive@f68cca701417 cli/lang/rust.mjs (wireLine lines 279-289, helpers lines 244-278, NARROWED_JSON line 57), canon.mjs encode for the f64 bits; selfhost seam seams/wire_line.mjs.
